//? Title: Kosaraju SCCs through actual two-pass DFS events
//? Subtitle: Finishing order, transpose assignments and the original eight-vertex graph
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A directed graph can contain many cycles, but vertices belong together only when
they can reach each other in both directions. Strongly connected components
partition the graph into those maximal groups and leave an acyclic condensation
graph. Kosaraju finds the partition with two iterative DFS passes. We inspect
actual exits and transpose discoveries, retain the original eight-vertex graph,
and check the partition against complete mutual reachability rather than relying
only on agreement with another SCC algorithm.
)

\toc

> Why the second pass uses reverse finishing order

Collapse each mutually reachable group to a condensation vertex. The
condensation is acyclic: a directed cycle of distinct groups would make their
members mutually reachable and merge them. For a condensation edge C→D, the
largest first-pass finishing time in C exceeds that in D. If DFS enters C first
it can finish reachable D before C; if D was already visited it already
finished. Thus the next unassigned maximum-finishing component has no incoming
edge from another remaining component in the original graph. In the transpose it
cannot escape to an unassigned component, while all its members remain mutually
reachable. A transpose DFS therefore assigns exactly that SCC; induction handles
the rest.

Roots of the first pass are ascending original vertex ids, edges follow caller
order and exits append to finish_order. Transpose rows are built in original
source order. The second pass uses reverse exits and assigns at discovery,
before stack processing. Actual numeric labels follow this root order and
topologically order cross-component edges, rather than denoting minimum vertex
ids. All state is iterative; no recursion limit is required.

> Three vertices: actual exits and assignments

In0→1→0 with1→2, the first two vertices form one SCC and vertex2 forms another.
The diagram summarizes actual producing finish_order and labels; the table
records real operations in order.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kosaraju_passes.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::kosaraju_trace(3, {{1}, {0, 2}, {}});
    std::print("| actual ordinal | operation code | vertex or source | edge target, minus1 if none | actual " "component, minus1 if first pass |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.events.size(); ++i) {
        const auto &e = t.events[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, int(e.kind), e.u, e.v,
                   e.component);
    }
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("first", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual first-pass exits\n{}, {}, {}", t.finish_order[0],
                       t.finish_order[1], t.finish_order[2])).node(
        "second", {7, 0},
        std::format("Actual transpose labels\n0:{}, 1:{}, 2:{}", t.component[0],
                    t.component[1], t.component[2])).edge("first", "second").save_svg("build/kosaraju_passes.svg");
}
```
