> Independent mutual reachability controls

A Boolean transitive closure provides an independent small-graph oracle: u and v
share an SCC exactly when reach[u][v] and reach[v][u]. The complete census below
checks every directed three-vertex graph including self-loops. Parallel edges
are separate entries for work, while neither loops nor duplicates change the
mutual-reachability definition. An empty graph returns an empty partition.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kosaraju_census.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<std::size_t> counts(4);
    for (int mask = 0; mask < 512; ++mask) {
        std::vector<std::vector<int>> a(3);
        bool reach[3][3]{};
        for (int u = 0; u < 3; ++u) {
            reach[u][u] = true;
            for (int v = 0; v < 3; ++v)
                if (mask & (1 << (u * 3 + v))) {
                    a[u].push_back(v);
            reach[u][v] = true;
                }
        }
        for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k)
            for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
                for (int j = 0; j < 3; ++j)
                    reach[i][j] = reach[i][j] || (reach[i][k] && reach[k][j]);
        const auto out = dm::algorithms::kosaraju_analyze(3, a);
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
            for (int j = 0; j < 3; ++j)
                if ((out.component[i] == out.component[j]) !=
                    (reach[i][j] && reach[j][i]))
                    throw std::runtime_error("mutual reachability mismatch");
        ++counts[out.work.second_roots];
    }
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| actual component count | complete three-vertex graph census |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int c = 1; c <= 3; ++c) {
        std::print("| {} | {} |\n", c, counts[c]);
        x.push_back(c);
        y.push_back(counts[c]);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.bars(x, y, "graphs", {37, 99, 235}, .6).y_limits(-10, 400).title(
        "All 512 directed three-vertex graphs checked").x_label(
        "actual SCC count").y_label("number of graphs").save_svg(
        "build/kosaraju_census.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual component count | complete three-vertex graph census |
| ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 144 |
| 2 | 168 |
| 3 | 200 |
\image(figures/kosaraju_census.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Complete finite graph census checked by independent Boolean transitive closure.)
\alttext(Bars count actual native component partitions by component count; every pairwise membership decision is checked against mutual reachability.)

> Checking actual linear work

Each first-pass adjacency entry is examined once, each transpose entry once,
each vertex discovered/finished once and each vertex assigned once. Therefore
the event capture has3n+2E actual operations. The selected edge-test sum is2E.
Transpose construction, validation, stack operations, copies and elapsed time
are excluded from those counters. Including them, the algorithm still takes
O(n+E) work and storage. A complete trace additionally owns the original graph
and actual output/event structures.

Adding the closing edge of a directed chain merges every vertex into one SCC,
but does not change the linear scan property. These are separate complete chain
and cycle runs, not partial executions reused as histories.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kosaraju_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| n | original edge entries | chain SCC count | cycle SCC count | actual cycle edge tests, " "both passes | expected twice E |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int n : {16, 64, 256, 1024, 4096}) {
        std::vector<std::vector<int>> a(n);
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; ++i)
            a[i].push_back(i + 1);
        const auto chain = dm::algorithms::kosaraju_analyze(n, a);
        a.back().push_back(0);
        const auto cycle = dm::algorithms::kosaraju_analyze(n, a);
        const auto tests =
            cycle.work.first_edge_tests + cycle.work.second_edge_tests;
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, n,
                   chain.work.second_roots, cycle.work.second_roots, tests,
                   2 * n);
        x.push_back(std::log2(n));
        y.push_back(std::log2(tests));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, y, "actual", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, y, "", {37, 99, 235}, 4).title("Actual two-pass cycle edge tests equal twice E").x_label("log2(n)").y_label("log2(actual edge examinations)").save_svg("build/kosaraju_work.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | original edge entries | chain SCC count | cycle SCC count | actual cycle edge tests, both passes | expected twice E |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 16 | 16 | 16 | 1 | 32 | 32 |
| 64 | 64 | 64 | 1 | 128 | 128 |
| 256 | 256 | 256 | 1 | 512 | 512 |
| 1024 | 1024 | 1024 | 1 | 2048 | 2048 |
| 4096 | 4096 | 4096 | 1 | 8192 | 8192 |
\image(figures/kosaraju_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual two-pass edge work on complete chain/cycle experiments.)
\alttext(The actual cycle scan count equals twice its input edge count; component counts show the effect of one closing edge.)

> Scope and verification

The complete graph must have n nonnegative vertices, exactly n rows and every
endpoint in range, checked before vertex access or copies. No global graph-size
optional cap applies to scalar/count-only calls. Trace capture admits original
n+E records before copying and each producing event before its operation. The
default million-entry ceiling excludes transpose/order/label copies, individual
fields, capacities and bytes/RAM; zero disables only that optional cap. All
output structures own their values and no replay is used.

Independent transitive closure and separately evaluated operation order
checked500 directed multigraphs and45812 actual events, including full
original/transpose edge multiplicity, exits and assignments. Frozen valid-input
labels matched2000 additional graphs exactly. Native tests exhaust all4096
loop-free directed four-vertex graphs, cover the original example and test
a100000-vertex iterative chain/cycle, complete input rejection, quotas and
ownership. The ten-report shared build passed141 native CTest cases,45 directly
instrumented address/undefined-behaviour sanitizer cases,90 compiled/executed
public examples,412/412 scoped file definitions plus11/11 changed
lagged-Fibonacci engine definitions, and ten representative clangd hovers. All
four complete report programs executed and all four native figures were visually
reviewed. Receipts and repeatable checks are retained under
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-184-193/`.

> References

M. Sharir(1981),
[A strong-connectivity algorithm and its applications in data flow analysis|https://doi.org/10.1016/0898-1221(81)90008-0],
Computers and Mathematics with Applications7(1),67–72. The two-pass algorithm is
commonly named Kosaraju–Sharir; actual native iterative ordering is specified
above.
